WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.120
- I don't value people's judgment.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.730
I think people struggle with judgment.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.130
If you're judging me, you're weak.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:08.963
Not me.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.713
You've got your perspective.

00:00:13.594 --> 00:00:16.255
(crowd cheers)

00:00:16.255 --> 00:00:17.088
I just want to be happy.

00:00:17.088 --> 00:00:18.776
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:18.776 --> 00:00:21.359
(upbeat music)

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.690
- [Woman] Thank you very much.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.560
I'm gonna walk up with this
cause it's really weird

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.520
to stand right at the back of
the room to ask a question.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.320
My question is around the,

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.640
you spoke about the wanting admiration,

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.776
which I think is a really
courageous thing to admit to.

00:00:35.776 --> 00:00:37.031
But in your book, Crushing It,

00:00:37.031 --> 00:00:40.870
you also talk about altruism
and how important that is.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:41.890
Where do you see the line between the two,

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.340
because my career is all
about helping charities

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.890
and nonprofits, and I'm
terrified at the idea

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.260
of losing myself in the spotlight.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.560
So where do you see the
line between the two?

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.450
- You know, I think the
thing that I talked about

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.150
very quickly up there
about I want health for you

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.880
and then I want self-awareness,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.993
I think the line is everybody
here has got a different line.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:02.640
I don't think anybody here should live

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.090
on anybody else's line.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.540
I think the key is to try
to figure yourself out.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:12.190
I didn't realize the reason
that I was a class clown

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.280
was because I wanted attention

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.900
because I was getting it at home.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.210
I think the line goes in a lot of places.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.650
I think that truth matters the most.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.920
There's a lot of people
that play in nonprofit.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.490
When I sit on nonprofit boards,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.140
and I realize that people join that board

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.310
to get access to me for business purposes,

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.400
that was a real eye-opener to me.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.314
And so, I think everybody
has their own line.

00:01:33.314 --> 00:01:36.500
But I think it's important to expose

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.250
what you're selfish about,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.850
because so many people
try to position themselves

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.490
as selfless at all causes,
and that's just not human.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.700
I think admiration doesn't
feel very courageous to me,

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.610
because the way I process it is,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.480
I'm desperate to bring you more value

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.150
than you bring to me
so that I have leverage

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.570
of your admiration, which, by virtue,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.120
feels like you're just
wanting to be a good person

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.180
for the sake of something that's in you.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.700
So, I just think that
you have to draw the line

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.550
where you draw the line.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:10.210
And, listen, if you're naturally
private, that's super fine.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.130
My question is why, though.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.850
I think one of the biggest reasons people

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:17.193
are naturally private is
cause they're hiding things.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.780
They want two things.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.440
They want stuff, but they also want

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.090
to keep certain things secrets.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.000
And so, I just think you
have to be thoughtful.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.470
If you're not honest with yourself,

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.400
you're fundamentally finished.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.290
And I think that's what I'm pushing on.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:31.123
- [Woman] Awesome, thank you so much.

00:02:31.123 --> 00:02:32.597
- You got it.

00:02:32.597 --> 00:02:34.850
(audience claps)

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.610
- [Man] I have two,
originally I had one question,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:40.217
gonna ask two cause the first
one's gonna be pretty quick.

00:02:40.217 --> 00:02:41.945
Trying to be brave there.

00:02:41.945 --> 00:02:43.235
(laughs)

00:02:43.235 --> 00:02:45.479
The first one is if you were to target

00:02:45.479 --> 00:02:49.128
the C-level executives within
a channel, what would you use?

00:02:49.128 --> 00:02:49.961
- LinkedIn.

00:02:49.961 --> 00:02:51.800
LinkedIn, if you're in B2B,

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.810
or you're trying to
get to business people,

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:54.643
I am blown away.

00:02:54.643 --> 00:02:56.800
I actually tweeted this the other day.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.390
I'm just stunned how much

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.200
of a content platform LinkedIn has become.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.830
Because, for a lot of us here,

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.760
LinkedIn was a utility for recruiting

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.460
and just reaching out to people.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.340
Using LinkedIn is not spamming
people on LinkedIn Messaging.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.100
Using LinkedIn is using LinkedIn the way

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.720
you use Facebook or Twitter,

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.430
putting out meaningful content.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.733
It finds itself to the
right people in that world.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.967
- [Man] Second question is,

00:03:22.967 --> 00:03:24.320
in the intro part of a journey,

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.417
how important do you think your purpose,

00:03:27.517 --> 00:03:29.038
or whatever you're gonna call it,

00:03:29.038 --> 00:03:30.600
is it to stay the course?

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.000
- I think being happy,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.460
and that's what I think
all these words, passion,

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:40.460
being happy is imperative
because it takes so many hours.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.980
It takes so many hours
to beat the other people

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.470
to get yours, kind of thing,

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:50.093
or just to take advantage
of the abundance.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.300
I just think an enormous amount of people

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.810
do it for the money,

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.230
which has nothing to do with happiness.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:04.760
And so, I think purpose, passion, process.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:08.423
I get such a high out of the tough part.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.400
I like the bad stuff.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.030
I like the setbacks.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:17.120
I'm actually unbelievably
addicted to the negativities

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.305
of running a business, the hard parts.

00:04:20.305 --> 00:04:21.851
- [Man] Can that give you
part of your goals as well?

00:04:21.851 --> 00:04:23.639
Cause when you like the process,

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:25.505
sometimes you just get it.

00:04:25.505 --> 00:04:27.643
- When you love the process,
you've achieved your goal.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.180
You have to understand, the money, for me,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.480
is the collateral to loving the process.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:35.880
Even the way you asked that question,

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.580
you're looking for more
money than I'm looking for.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.857
And, by the way, that
doesn't mean I'm right.

00:04:40.857 --> 00:04:43.620
Just even the way you asked the question,

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:45.830
I've already won by putting
myself in the process.

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.520
All the other part is secondary, got it?

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.220
So you're not gonna to
be in it for the process.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:54.230
I mean that, which is
okay, it really is okay.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:55.630
The key is trying to figure out

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:57.520
why you want the other stuff.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.993
It's okay to want money, why?

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:03.900
A lot of people love the idea of money

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.250
so they don't have to work.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.263
That usually doesn't work out.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.081
- [Man] Alright, next question.

00:05:14.081 --> 00:05:16.550
Let's keep the questions
to only one please.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.381
- [Man] Hi, Gary, I'm Joel.

00:05:18.381 --> 00:05:19.320
I'm 31 years old this year.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.077
I just want to ask,

00:05:21.077 --> 00:05:22.767
I know you know this already,

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:26.039
but national service is
like a compulsory thing

00:05:26.039 --> 00:05:29.746
for all males in this
country, so, military service.

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:32.219
So I'm working this out,
sometime mid next year.

00:05:32.219 --> 00:05:36.190
Entering occupations, and
again, bigger picture like,

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:39.310
how do you work around,
putting your whole life on hold

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:40.930
for pretty much a few years?

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.380
- Because you have no choice.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:47.260
- [Man] Yeah, and then
the world's going so fast

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:48.093
when I finish in three years--

00:05:48.093 --> 00:05:49.390
- The world will be there for you

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:51.653
when you're done doing
your military service.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:54.410
Just because you don't want to do it,

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.480
or because you're excited to do this stuff

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.520
that you want to be doing
but you're mandatory,

00:05:58.520 --> 00:05:59.910
you were born in Singapore, bro.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:02.319
You're going to the fucking military.

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:04.320
(audience laughs)

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.930
That's just the way it is and that's that.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.460
And so, there's pros
and cons to everything.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.643
You could have been born
in an indigenous country.

00:06:12.643 --> 00:06:14.970
There's a lot worse places

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.460
to win the lottery than Singapore.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.790
You, like Israel and
other places in the world,

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:21.620
you're going to have to pay your services.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.540
But this whole thought
that I'm going to miss out

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.230
on my time is ludicrous.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.381
If you're good enough,

00:06:27.381 --> 00:06:29.020
if you're good enough,

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:30.550
the years that you were in the military

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.703
aren't going to be the reasons you lost.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.343
Don't use it as an excuse, bro.

00:06:37.268 --> 00:06:38.759
- [Joel] Alright, thank you.

00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:40.176
- You're welcome.

00:06:41.335 --> 00:06:43.866
- [Man] Hey, Gary, it's
me again, from India.

00:06:43.866 --> 00:06:45.534
